## README

This is an introduction to the analysis of first registration of personal vehicles from Estonia, specifically focusing on the year 2026 in the context of most popular car brands. The dataset is composed of car brands from different manufacturers (BMW,...,Ford) and a number of registrations (1,...,254) corresponding to a year of manufacture (2006,...,2026).

The dataset was taken from the Statistics Estonia website: https://andmed.eesti.ee/datasets/infoleht-%28esmaste-ja-uute-soidukite-statistika%29, where only one sheet of a larger dataset was used to observe a specific proble and older vehicles were disincluded.

The problem is "How many vehicles are first registered from a period of 20 years and how probable it is to choose to register a certain car brand. In this study we'll be focusing on conditional probability, where probability "P" is dependent on "A" brand given "B" year, therefore the notation is following P(A|B). For the probability model we're trying to do we're using the following equation: P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B), which states that "Probability of brand is dependent on B the year of manufacture." To do this for each year separately would be tedius and require a lot of time so therefore we start with firstly taking all of the registered cars together and making a table with different brands.


```{r}
#Reading in the document 

Data <- read.csv(file = "Soidukid.csv")

#Changing data to fit the premise of our analysis better, firstly decreasing the nr of columns and increasing rows. Also corresponding years of registration and brands are now together in the same columns, making the analysis on probability much easier to work with for the whole dataset, not by doing each year separately

library(tidyr)
library(dplyr)

Data_long <- Data %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = starts_with("X20"),
    names_to = "Year",
    values_to = "Count"
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    Year = as.numeric(gsub("X", "", Year))
  )

```

Data_year_prob <- Data_long %>%
  group_by(Year) %>%
  mutate(Prob = Count / sum(Count, na.rm = TRUE))

Data_brand_prob <- Data_long %>%
  group_by(Mark) %>%
  mutate(Prob = Count / sum(Count, na.rm = TRUE))


library(ggplot2)
  ggplot(Data_brand_prob, aes(x = Year, y = Prob, color = Mark)) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_point() +
  labs(
    title = "Brand Share Over Time",
    x = "Year",
    y = "P(Brand | Year)"
  )
  
  ggplot(Data_year_prob, aes(x = Year, y = Mark, fill = Prob)) +
  geom_tile() +
  labs(
    title = "Brand Probability Heatmap",
    fill = "P(Brand | Year)"
  )

